          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The principle of
            %em identity of indiscernibles
            states that two objects are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identical"} identical
            if they have all the same
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/properties"} properties
          %p
            This is also known as “Leibniz’s Law”, in honor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottfried+Leibniz"} Gottfried Leibniz
            (not to be confused with his
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+rule"} product rule
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 40px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/LeibnizOnIndiscernibles-Phil7SecXIX.jpg", :width => "490px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                G. W. Leibniz around 1700, from
                %a{:href => "#Gerhard1890"} Gerhard 1890, p. 288
          %p#LeibnizWroteTheFollowing
            Concretely, Leibniz wrote the following, (in translation from
            %a{:href => "#Lewis18"} Lewis 1918, p. 373
            with original Latin terms in parenthesis; see also
            %a{:href => "#Cartwright71"} Cartwright 1971, p. 119
            and
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#GriesSchneider98"} Gries &amp; Schneider 1998
          %blockquote
            %p
              Two terms are the
              %em same
              (
              %em> eadem
              ) if one can be substituted for the other without altering the truth of any statement (
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/salva+veritate"} salva veritate
              ). If we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              enters into some true proposition, and the substitution of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              wherever it appears results in a new proposition that is likewise true, and if this can be done for every proposition, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              are said to be the same; …
          %p
            This sentence of Leibniz in fact closes with a statement of the converse, often known as the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+substitutivity"}> principle of substitutivity
            ” but which may deserve to be called the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em indiscernibility of identicals
          %blockquote
            %p
              … conversely, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              are the same, they can be substituted for one another.
          %p The paragraph ends with the assertion that
          %blockquote
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              are, of course, said to be the same
          %p
            This is what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grundlage+der+gesamten+Wissenschaftslehre"} Fichte 1794
            later called the “first, absolutely unconditioned principle” and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Hegel 1812-
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic#875"} called
            the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+law+of+thought"}> first law of thought
            ”.
          %p
            From the perspective of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+law+of+thought"} first law of thought
            = succeed "-rule" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction"} term introduction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            = succeed "-rule," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transport"} transport
            = succeed "-rule" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J-rule"}> J-rule
            ”). For more on this see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#InHomotopyTypeTheory"} below
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rule"} inference rule
            for the identity of indiscernables is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} set
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mo ∀
                        %mi P
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo .
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⇔
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                      %mi y
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{set}}{\Gamma, x \in A, y \in A \vdash \left(\forall P \in \mathcal{P}(A).P(x) \iff P(y)\right) \implies x =_A y \; \mathrm{true}}
          %h2#InHomotopyTypeTheory In dependent type theory
          %h3#leibnizs_identity_of_indiscernibles Leibniz’s identity of indiscernibles
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+all+propositions"} type of all propositions
            and thus
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            on a type. In addition, unlike with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idOfInd
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idOfInd}_A:\prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \left(\prod_{P:\mathcal{P}(A)} P(x) \simeq P(y)\right) \to (x =_A y)
          %p
            This is because the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            has more than one element, and thus indiscernibles could be identified in more than one way. Instead, the identity of indiscenibles for type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is given by the dependent function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idOfInd
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idOfInd}_A:\prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} (x =_A y) \simeq \left(\prod_{P:\mathcal{P}(A)} P(x) \simeq P(y)\right)
          %p
            More concretely, for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+substitution"} principle of substitution
            is always true because by induction on reflexivity, one could define a function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idToInd
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idToInd}_A(x, y):(x =_A y) \to \left(\prod_{P:\mathcal{P}(A)} P(x) \simeq P(y)\right)
          %p
            The identity of indiscernibles is simply the statement that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idToInd
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idToInd}_A(x, y)
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+types"} equivalence of types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+orders"} partial orders
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kolmogorov+topological+spaces"} Kolmogorov topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+categories"} skeletal categories
            in terms of equivalences between identity types and a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+endorelation"} binary endorelation
          %p
            If we define the local membership relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in_A P
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %mi P
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in_A P \coloneqq P(x)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idOfInd
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idOfInd}_A:\prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} (x =_A y) \simeq \left(\prod_{P:\mathcal{P}(A)} (x \in_A P) \simeq (y \in_A P)\right)
          %p
            This is similar to but not identical to the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"} axiom of extensionality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} extax
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{extax}_A:\prod_{P:\mathcal{P}(A)} \prod_{Q:\mathcal{P}(A)} (P =_{\mathcal{P}(A)} Q) \simeq \left(\prod_{x:A} (x \in_A P) \simeq (x \in_A Q)\right)
          %p
            If Leibniz’s identity of indiscenibles is true for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a set, because the type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{P:\mathcal{P}(A)} P(x) \simeq P(y)
          %p
            is always a proposition, in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+function+extensionality"} weak function extensionality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+types"} equivalence of types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x =_A y
            is also a proposition for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a set. Thus, if Leibniz’s identity of indiscernibles is true for all types
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            becomes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+type+theory"} set-level type theory
            with Leibniz’s identity of indiscernibles as an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+set+truncation"} axiom of set truncation
          %p
            On the other hand, one does not necessarily need Leibniz’s identity of indiscernibles to be true for all types
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ind
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A, y:A \vdash \mathrm{ind}_A(x, y) \coloneqq \prod_{P:\mathcal{P}(A)} P(x) \simeq P(y)
          %p
            is always an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ind
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/\mathrm{ind}_A
          %p as the type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ind
                    %mi A
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ind
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/\mathrm{ind}_A \coloneqq \sum_{Q:\mathcal{P}(A)} \left[\sum_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} Q(x) \simeq \mathrm{ind}_A(x, y)\right]
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
            terms, this means that every type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            with its power set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}(A)
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R0-space"} R0-space
            and the quotient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ind
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/\mathrm{ind}_A
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T1-space"} T1-space
          %h3#typetheoretic_identity_of_indiscernibles Type-theoretic identity of indiscernibles
          %p
            For a version of the identity of indiscernibles which applies to not just sets, but other non-set types, one would have to go from quantifying over predicates to quantifying over
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+families"} type families
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is given by the dependent function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idOfInd
                    %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idOfInd}_U:\prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} (x =_A y) \simeq \left(\prod_{P:A \to U} P(x) \simeq P(y)\right)
          %p
            The type-theoretic identity of indiscernibles is similar to but not identical to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski+universes"} Tarski universes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U, T)
            quantifies over
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A:U \vdash T(A)
            for Tarski universes, while the identity of indiscernables for type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            quantifies over
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            Alternatively, if we want the usual identity of indiscernibles to apply to non-set types, then we have to restrict the identity types to precisely the identity types which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equalities"} propositional equalities
            in the sense of having a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unique"} unique
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identification"} identification
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idOfInd
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isContr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idOfInd}:\prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{isContr}(\mathrm{Id}_A(x, y)) \simeq \prod_{x:A \to \mathrm{Prop}} P(x) \simeq P(y)
          %h3#other_versions Other versions
          %p
            In addition, extensionality principles like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+extensionality"} propositional extensionality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            (“typal extensionality”) are naturally regarded as a stronger form of identity of indiscernibles. In particular, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/consistency"} consistency
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            means that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            without univalence, one cannot define any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            that provably distinguishes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+homotopy+type+theory"} equivalent
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
          %p
            “There are several ways to think about the axiom of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2013/11/categories_for_the_working_phi.html"} nCafé
          %p
            On the other hand, the converse substitution/substitutivity principle of “indiscernibility of identicals” is expressd by
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transport"} transport
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %h2#InPointSetTopology In point-set topology
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
            or geometrical version of the idea of identity of indiscernibles is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axioms"} separation
          %p#InTopologicalSpacesMoreInDetail More in detail:
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            one might declare that “topological observations” about the space are questions of the form: “Which points are contained in a given open subset?”, and hence that two points are “discernible”, namely “topologically distinct”, if there exists an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
            that contains one but not the other. With this convention, the condition that “indiscernibles be identical” is equivalently the condition that the topological space satisfies the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separation axiom
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kolmogorov+space"} Kolmogorov space
            %a{:href => "Kolmogorov+topological+space#KolmogorovQuotient"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
              \-reflection
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            under which identity becomes indiscernibility, in this sense.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dominance"} dominance
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \subseteq \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(A)
            denote the type of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
            of the type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to \Sigma
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separation axiom
            for type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is given by the dependent function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0:\prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} (x =_A y) \simeq \left(\prod_{P:\mathcal{O}(A)} P(x) \simeq P(y)\right)
          %p
            Leibniz’s identity of indiscernibles is simply the
            = succeed "-separation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+all+propositions"} type of all propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is the dominance and where the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            are the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
          %p
            In addition, given an type universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is simply the
            = succeed "-separation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                    %mi U
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}_U \coloneqq \sum_{A:U} \mathrm{isProp}(A)
            is the dominance, and where the
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            are the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                      %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}_U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+law+of+thought"} first law of thought
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transport"} transport
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"} axiom of extensionality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+set+truncation"} axiom of set truncation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T0-space"}> T0-space
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T1-space"} T1-space
          %h2#references References
          %p Leibniz’s original paragraph (from an unpublished manuscript probably written after 1683) is reproduced in the Latin Original in
          %ul
            %li#Gerhard1890
              K. Gerhard (ed.), Section XIX,
              %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/diephilosophisc00gerhgoog/page/228/mode/2up"} p. 228
              in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Die philosophischen Schriften von Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/diephilosophisc00gerhgoog/page/n7/mode/2up"} archive.org
          %p and in English translation in:
          %ul
            %li#Lewis18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clarence+I.+Lewis"}> Clarence I. Lewis
              , Appendix (p. 373) of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Survey of Symbolic Logic
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LewisAppendix-LeibnizIndiscernibles.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p and further discussed in:
          %ul
            %li#Cartwright71
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Cartwright"}> Richard Cartwright
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Identity and Substitutivity
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Identity and Individuation
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Cartwright-IdentityAndSubstitutivity.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#GriesSchneider98
              %p
                David Gries, Fred Schneider,
                %em Formalizations Of Substitution Of Equals For Equals
                (1998) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://ecommons.cornell.edu/bitstream/handle/1813/7340/98-1686.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y"}> pdf
                ,
                %a{:href => "https://ecommons.cornell.edu/handle/1813/7340"} ecommons:1813/7340
          %p Leibniz’s original terminology “salva veritate” for the substitution principle is prominently used in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W.+V.+O.+Quine"}> W. V. O. Quine
              , §3 of
              %em Two Dogmas of Empiricism
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %em Three grades of modal involvement
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quintessence – Basic readings from the philosophy of W. V. Quine
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.hup.harvard.edu/catalog.php?isbn=9780674027558"} ISBN:9780674027558
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_of_indiscernibles"} Identity of indiscernibles
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salva_veritate"} Salva veritate
            %li
              %p
                Standord Encyclopedia of Philosophy,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-indiscernible/"} The Identity of Indiscernibles
          %p
            Formalization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"}> Univalent Foundations Project
                , section 1.12 of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
                (2013)
            %li#Favonia17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Favonia"}> Favonia
                , appendix B of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher-Dimensional Types in the Mechanization of Homotopy Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~kuenbanh/files/thesis.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The understanding of “indiscernibility of identicals” as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transport"} transport
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is made explicit in:
          %ul
            %li#LadymanPresnell15
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Ladyman"}> James Ladyman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stuart+Presnell"} Stuart Presnell
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Identity in Homotopy Type Theory, Part I: The Justification of Path Induction
                %strong 23
                3 (2015) 386–406 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/philmat/nkv014"}> doi:10.1093/philmat/nkv014
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://philsci-archive.pitt.edu/11079/1/Identity_in_HTT_public.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benedikt+Ahrens"}> Benedikt Ahrens
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paige+Randall+North"} Paige Randall North
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Univalent foundations and the equivalence principle
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Reflections on the Foundations of Mathematics
                %strong 407
                Springer (2019) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.01892"}> arXiv:2202.01892
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-15655-8"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-15655-8
